      "updated": 1771404752853,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Data Defination Language (DDL) Commands\n    1. The `COLUMN` keyword is optional in `ALTER` commands.\n    2. You cannot change the datatype from `string to decimal` unless all the values are in integer just like python, but you can change from int to string, infact you can change anything to string\n    3. Difference between trucate and drop and delete from table_name (without where statment) -> drop will removes the entire data with table but truncate will only deletes the data.\n    \n    → There are important points regarding `EXISTS` and `WHERE` clause.\n\nData Manipulation Language (DML) Commands\n    1. Different ways of insertion\n        - Inserting with specificied columns - If you want to add values only in a specific set of columns then, you need to pass `NULL` as a value for other column.\n        - Inserting from another table\n    2. The `UPDATE` and `DELETE` commands results reflect over entire dataset if there is no `WHERE` clause.\n    3. SELECT is a DQL/DML command\n    \n    Creating new column with default values\n        SELECT  *, 'samsung' AS 'phone'\n        FROM table_name;\n\n    → `DEFAULT` and `AUTO INCREMENT` checks \n\nGROUP BY Statments\n    1. ORDER BY: single column, multiple columns\n    2. LIMIT Clause: 3 syntexes\n        a. LIMIT with just one number\n        b. LIMIT with 2 numbers [first number is offset and second number is number of rows]\n        c. LIMIT with OFFSET\n    3. NOTE: If you are performing a `GROUP BY` operation over a column which contains `NULL` values then `NULL` is considered as a seperate, stand-alone group.\n    4. GROUP BY: with more than one columns\n    5. HAVING clause\n\n    → SELECT *, SUM(id) FROM zomato.order_details; -- * expands to all columns from the table (including id, which is already referenced separately). The aggregate functions (SUM, AVG, etc.)\nare computed over the entire table as a single group. The nonaggregated columns (like all columns from the table) are not part of the GROUP BY clause, and there is no GROUP BY at all.\n-- Error Code: 1140. In aggregated query without GROUP BY, expression #1 of SELECT list contains nonaggregated column 'zomato.order_details.id'; this is incompatible with\nsql_mode=only_full_group_by\n\nJoins\n    1. CROSS JOIN - This join is basically used when there is no column having same name in the joining tables otherwise it becomes inner join.\n    2. INNER JOIN\n        Note: You cannot just write the name of the column even though the name is same in both the tables. -> Error Code: 1052. Column 'id' in on clause is ambiguous\n    3. RIGHT / LEFT JOIN - The values corrosponding to the records from right table which is not involved in the group by there values for the features of the left table considered as NULL.\n    4. FULL OUTER JOIN - using `UNION`\n    5. SELF JOIN\n    6. Set Operations:\n        1. UNION\n        2. UNION ALL\n        3. INTERSECT\n        4. EXCEPT\n\n    → `JOIN` without JOIN statment - In below SQL query each id from T1 will be compared with each id in T2 and for each `true` result it will return the id in the table.",
      "fontSize": 22.442958286830358,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Data Defination Language (DDL) Commands\n    1. The `COLUMN` keyword is optional in `ALTER` commands.\n    2. You cannot change the datatype from `string to decimal` unless all the values are in integer just like python, but you can change from int to string, infact you can change anything to string\n    3. Difference between trucate and drop and delete from table_name (without where statment) -> drop will removes the entire data with table but truncate will only deletes the data.\n    \n    → There are important points regarding `EXISTS` and `WHERE` clause.\n\nData Manipulation Language (DML) Commands\n    1. Different ways of insertion\n        - Inserting with specificied columns - If you want to add values only in a specific set of columns then, you need to pass `NULL` as a value for other column.\n        - Inserting from another table\n    2. The `UPDATE` and `DELETE` commands results reflect over entire dataset if there is no `WHERE` clause.\n    3. SELECT is a DQL/DML command\n    \n    Creating new column with default values\n        SELECT  *, 'samsung' AS 'phone'\n        FROM table_name;\n\n    → `DEFAULT` and `AUTO INCREMENT` checks \n\nGROUP BY Statments\n    1. ORDER BY: single column, multiple columns\n    2. LIMIT Clause: 3 syntexes\n        a. LIMIT with just one number\n        b. LIMIT with 2 numbers [first number is offset and second number is number of rows]\n        c. LIMIT with OFFSET\n    3. NOTE: If you are performing a `GROUP BY` operation over a column which contains `NULL` values then `NULL` is considered as a seperate, stand-alone group.\n    4. GROUP BY: with more than one columns\n    5. HAVING clause\n\n    → SELECT *, SUM(id) FROM zomato.order_details; -- * expands to all columns from the table (including id, which is already referenced separately). The aggregate functions (SUM, AVG, etc.) are computed over the entire table as a single group. The nonaggregated columns (like all columns from the table) are not part of the GROUP BY clause, and there is no GROUP BY at all.\n-- Error Code: 1140. In aggregated query without GROUP BY, expression #1 of SELECT list contains nonaggregated column 'zomato.order_details.id'; this is incompatible with sql_mode=only_full_group_by\n\nJoins\n    1. CROSS JOIN - This join is basically used when there is no column having same name in the joining tables otherwise it becomes inner join.\n    2. INNER JOIN\n        Note: You cannot just write the name of the column even though the name is same in both the tables. -> Error Code: 1052. Column 'id' in on clause is ambiguous\n    3. RIGHT / LEFT JOIN - The values corrosponding to the records from right table which is not involved in the group by there values for the features of the left table considered as NULL.\n    4. FULL OUTER JOIN - using `UNION`\n    5. SELF JOIN\n    6. Set Operations:\n        1. UNION\n        2. UNION ALL\n        3. INTERSECT\n        4. EXCEPT\n\n    → `JOIN` without JOIN statment - In below SQL query each id from T1 will be compared with each id in T2 and for each `true` result it will return the id in the table.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    }
  ],
